    "id": "CEH-V13-CQ-00-027",
    "chapterIndex": 0,
    "text": "An ethical hacker wants to perform a highly covert port scan against a target network (`192.168.10.0/24`) without exposing the tester's true IP address. The tester searches for an idle host (\"zombie\") on the network that utilizes predictable IP ID sequence generation and experiences low background network traffic. Which Nmap scan syntax correctly executes an Idle Scan using the zombie host at IP `192.168.10.50` against target host `192.168.10.100`?",
    "choices": [
      "nmap -sS -p 80 192.168.10.50 --spoof 192.168.10.100",
      "nmap -sI 192.168.10.50 192.168.10.100",
      "nmap -sZ 192.168.10.50:192.168.10.100",
      "nmap -PN -sU --zombie 192.168.10.50 192.168.10.100"
    ],
    "correct": 1,
    "explanation": "Correct Choice Analysis: The `-sI` flag in Nmap initiates an Idle Scan (also known as a Zombie Scan). The syntax requires providing the zombie host IP address immediately following `-sI`, followed by the target host IP address (`nmap -sI <zombie host> <target host>`).\nDistractor Analysis:\n- Option A is incorrect because `-sS` performs a TCP SYN scan, and `--spoof` manually spoofs source IP addresses without leveraging the IP ID sequence tracking mechanism of an idle host.\n- Option C is incorrect because `-sZ` is an invalid, non-existent Nmap flag combination.\n- Option D is incorrect because `--zombie` is not a valid Nmap command flag.",
    "distractors": [
      "nmap -sS -p 80 192.168.10.50 --spoof 192.168.10.100",
      "nmap -sZ 192.168.10.50:192.168.10.100",
      "nmap -PN -sU --zombie 192.168.10.50 192.168.10.100"
    ]
