    "id": "CEH-V13-CQ-00-052",
    "chapterIndex": 0,
    "text": "A Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is performing quantitative risk analysis for a primary database server valued at $500,000. Threat intelligence indicates that a major database corruption incident occurs statistically once every 5 years (Annualized Rate of Occurrence, ARO = 0.2). If a corruption event occurs, the Exposure Factor (EF) is estimated at 40%. What is the Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE) for this specific risk asset?",
    "choices": [
      "$200,000",
      "$40,000",
      "$100,000",
      "$50,000"
    ],
    "correct": 1,
    "explanation": "Correct Choice Analysis: Quantitative risk analysis calculates Single Loss Expectancy as $SLE = \\text{Asset Value (AV)} \\times \\text{Exposure Factor (EF)}$, yielding $500,000 \\times 0.40 = 200,000$. The Annualized Loss Expectancy is calculated as $ALE = SLE \\times ARO$, yielding $200,000 \\times 0.2 = 40,000$.\nDistractor Analysis:\n- Option A ($200,000) represents the Single Loss Expectancy (SLE), failing to multiply by the Annualized Rate of Occurrence (ARO).\n- Option C ($100,000) results from miscalculating ARO or applying half the asset value incorrectly.\n- Option D ($50,000) represents incorrect arithmetic division ($200,000 / 4$).",
