    "correct": 2,
    "explanation": "Correct Choice Analysis: When ICMP is blocked, the most reliable discovery method is to send TCP SYN (or ACK) packets to ports that are commonly open or at least filtered consistently. A SYN-ACK or RST response indicates a live host; lack of response can still be interpreted with multiple ports. This technique bypasses pure ICMP filtering.\nDistractor Analysis: Option A is incorrect because the firewall is known to drop ICMP Echo Requests, so a classic ping sweep will return almost no results. Option B is incorrect because ARP is a Layer-2 protocol and cannot be used from an external vantage point across the Internet. Option D is incorrect because UDP probes are slower, often rate-limited, and many firewalls simply drop the packets without generating ICMP unreachable messages.",
