    "id": "CEH-V13-CQ-009-046",
    "chapterIndex": 8,
    "text": "An organization implements DMARC in enforcement mode (p=reject) alongside SPF and DKIM for its email domain. What is the PRIMARY security benefit this configuration provides against social engineering attacks?",
    "choices": [
      "It prevents attackers from successfully spoofing the organization's own domain in phishing emails sent to others",
      "It automatically detects and blocks all vishing calls originating from spoofed caller IDs",
      "It encrypts all outbound email attachments to prevent data exfiltration",
      "It prevents employees from clicking malicious links regardless of the sender domain"
    ],
    "correct": 0, // 0-indexed integer corresponding to choices array
    "explanation": "Correct Choice Analysis: DMARC in enforcement mode, combined with SPF and DKIM, is correct because its primary function is to prevent attackers from successfully spoofing the organization's own domain when sending phishing emails (whether to external parties or internally), by instructing receiving mail servers to reject unauthenticated messages claiming to be from that domain.\nDistractor Analysis: It does not detect or block vishing calls, as DMARC is an email authentication protocol unrelated to voice/telephony communications. It does not encrypt outbound attachments; DMARC/SPF/DKIM are authentication mechanisms, not encryption technologies. It does not prevent employees from clicking malicious links from other, unrelated spoofed or lookalike domains that the organization does not control — its protection is scoped specifically to the organization's own authenticated domain.",
    "distractors": [
      "It automatically detects and blocks all vishing calls originating from spoofed caller IDs",
      "It encrypts all outbound email attachments to prevent data exfiltration",
      "It prevents employees from clicking malicious links regardless of the sender domain"
    ]
