    "id": "CEH-V13-CQ-020-002",
    "chapterIndex": 19,
    "text": "During a red team engagement, an analyst gains access to a legacy VPN gateway that always encrypts a known startup handshake banner before establishing a session. The analyst captures both the banner plaintext and the resulting ciphertext across multiple sessions to deduce properties of the encryption key. Which cryptanalytic model best describes this technique?",
    "choices": [
      "Chosen-Ciphertext Attack",
      "Chosen-Plaintext Attack",
      "Known-Plaintext Attack",
      "Ciphertext-Only Attack"
    ],
    "correct": 2,
    "explanation": "Correct Choice Analysis: A Known-Plaintext Attack (KPA) occurs when the attacker has access to both the plaintext and its corresponding ciphertext without being able to choose the plaintext themselves. Since the VPN banner is a fixed, known value that the analyst observes being encrypted, and the analyst never controls what is encrypted, this matches KPA exactly.\nDistractor Analysis: Option 0 (Chosen-Ciphertext Attack) is incorrect because it requires the attacker to submit ciphertext to a decryption oracle, which is not occurring here. Option 1 (Chosen-Plaintext Attack) is incorrect because the analyst did not select or influence what plaintext (the banner) gets encrypted; it was fixed by the system. Option 3 (Ciphertext-Only Attack) is incorrect because the analyst does have visibility into the plaintext banner text, which exceeds the ciphertext-only model.",
