    "id": "CEH-V13-CQ-020-081",
    "chapterIndex": 19,
    "text": "A penetration testing firm is contracted to assess a client's Active Directory environment. During the engagement, the team extracts NTLM password hashes from the NTDS.dit file and finds that no salting mechanism exists in NTLM's design, meaning identical passwords across different accounts always produce identical hash values. The team leverages this by cross-referencing hashes against a massive precomputed dataset optimized specifically for this hash format. What structural property of NTLM makes it fundamentally more susceptible to this precomputation technique compared to a properly salted modern hashing scheme like bcrypt?",
